# Obsidian OS - package set common to every flavor (first-light baseline).
#
# This list is deliberately MINIMAL and REAL: every entry is a package that
# exists in Debian trixie and that the system genuinely needs in order to boot
# under QEMU with the hardening overlays actually applied.
#
# Rules for this file:
#   - one package per line; `#` comments only; no version pins here.
#     Version pinning is a snapshot.debian.org change, tracked in ROADMAP.
#   - no entry may reference a package that does not exist in trixie.
#     `python3 scripts/validate-build.py --online` resolves every entry against
#     the real trixie Packages index, so a typo fails the gate in seconds
#     rather than 20 minutes into a build.
#   - offensive tooling never appears here, in any flavor. See docs/LEGAL.md and
#     docs/ADR-0002; the Pentester flavor ships no attack tooling at all.
#
# --- init / kernel / boot chain -------------------------------------------
systemd
systemd-sysv
linux-image-amd64
initramfs-tools
kmod
procps
udev

# --- mandatory access control + firewall (the hardening story) ------------
apparmor
apparmor-utils
apparmor-profiles
nftables

# --- core userspace the rest of the OS assumes exists ---------------------
dbus
sudo
ca-certificates
apt-utils
iproute2
iputils-ping
less
nano
vim-tiny
bash-completion
zram-tools
locales

# --- live-session plumbing (we are a live ISO) ----------------------------
live-boot
squashfs-tools

# --- base net stack: systemd-networkd + resolved -------------------------
# NOTE: there is no separate `systemd-networkd` package in trixie. The daemon is
# shipped by `systemd` itself and the `systemd-networkd.service` unit comes from
# it. Listing it as its own dependency fails package resolution -- caught by
# `validate-build.py --online`, which is precisely what that check is for.
#
# A headless serial console is what tests/boottest.sh reads. The kernel command
# line is assembled by build/build-iso.sh (see CMDLINE_EXTRA there, and
# overlays/<flavor>/etc/obsidian/<flavor>/cmdline.extra). It pins
# console=ttyS0,115200n8 last, so /dev/console lands on the captured line.
systemd-resolved
systemd-timesyncd
